/**
  @page guides-io-xdmf XDMF format
  @brief Using the XDMF format for visualization and time series data

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-io-xdmf-intro Introduction

  The eXtensible Data Model and Format (XDMF) is a data format designed for
  scientific computing. It uses a split-file architecture:

  - An **XML file** (`.xdmf`) that describes the structure of the data
  - One or more **HDF5 files** (`.h5`) that store the actual numerical data

  This design combines human-readable metadata with efficient binary storage,
  with mesh and field data stored separately from their description. XDMF files can be directly
  opened in [ParaView](https://www.paraview.org/) for visualization.

  @section guides-io-xdmf-class The XDMF class

  The @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF class provides a high-level interface for writing
  XDMF files. Output is organized by grids:

  1. Create an XDMF writer
  2. Associate a mesh with a named grid
  3. Add fields (scalar, vector, tensor) to the grid
  4. Write snapshots, optionally with time stamps

  @subsection guides-io-xdmf-class-basic Basic usage

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Geometry.h>
  #include <Rodin/Variational.h>
  #include <Rodin/IO/XDMF.h>

  using namespace Rodin;
  using namespace Rodin::Geometry;
  using namespace Rodin::Variational;

  int main()
  {
    Mesh mesh;
    mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});
    mesh.scale(1.0 / 15.0);

    P1 vh(mesh);
    GridFunction u(vh);
    u = [](const Geometry::Point& p) { return p.x() * p.y(); };

    IO::XDMF xdmf("MyOutput");
    xdmf.grid().setMesh(mesh).add("u", u);
    xdmf.write().flush();

    return 0;
  }
  @endcode

  This creates:
  - `MyOutput.xdmf` — XML metadata file (open this in ParaView)
  - `MyOutput.mesh.h5` — HDF5 file containing the mesh data
  - `MyOutput.u.000000.h5` — HDF5 file containing the field data

  @section guides-io-xdmf-guide-grids Working with grids

  A grid in XDMF associates a mesh with named fields. The writer returns a
  handle to the default grid or to a grid selected by name:

  @code{.cpp}
  IO::XDMF xdmf("Simulation");
  auto grid = xdmf.grid();          // default grid name
  auto domain = xdmf.grid("Domain");  // named grid
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-io-xdmf-guide-grids-mesh Setting the mesh

  Each grid must have a mesh associated with it before writing:

  @code{.cpp}
  auto grid = xdmf.grid();
  grid.setMesh(mesh);
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-io-xdmf-guide-grids-fields Adding fields

  Fields are added using the `add()` method. The field can be a named
  GridFunction or provided with an explicit name:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Option 1: Name the grid function and add it
  GridFunction u(vh);
  u.setName("velocity");
  grid.add(u);

  // Option 2: Provide a name when adding
  grid.add("pressure", p);
  @endcode

  Registration borrows the mesh and fields. Their spaces and coefficients
  must remain valid whenever a snapshot is written. After remeshing, construct
  the new spaces and register the current fields before the next write.

  @section guides-io-xdmf-static Static output

  For a single snapshot, call `write()` without
  a time parameter:

  @code{.cpp}
  IO::XDMF xdmf("StaticResult");
  xdmf.grid().setMesh(mesh).add("solution", u);
  xdmf.write().flush();
  @endcode

  @section guides-io-xdmf-temporal Time-dependent output

  Call `write(t).flush()` after updating the registered fields to record
  a snapshot at time @f$t@f$:

  @code{.cpp}
  IO::XDMF xdmf("TimeEvolution");
  auto grid = xdmf.grid();
  grid.setMesh(mesh);
  grid.add("u", u);

  for (size_t k = 0; k < numSteps; ++k)
  {
    Real t = k * dt;

    // Update your solution...
    u = [t](const Geometry::Point& p)
    {
      return std::sin(Math::Constants::pi() * p.x()) * std::exp(-t);
    };

    // Write snapshot at time t
    xdmf.write(t).flush();
  }
  @endcode

  ParaView's time controls step through
  the evolution of the solution.

  @subsection guides-io-xdmf-temporal-mesh Mesh policy

  By default, the mesh is considered static (exported once). Set it before
  the output loop and keep its geometry and topology unchanged. Calling
  `setMesh()` again resets the grid's static-mesh export state.
  For a mesh that changes over time, configure the writer
  to export the mesh at each time step:

  @code{.cpp}
  IO::XDMF xdmf("AdaptiveSim");
  auto grid = xdmf.grid();
  grid.setMesh(mesh, IO::XDMF::MeshPolicy::Transient);
  grid.add(u);
  @endcode

  @section guides-io-xdmf-fields Field types

  XDMF supports different types of fields:

  @subsection guides-io-xdmf-fields-scalar Scalar fields

  Scalar fields have one component per node:

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 vh(mesh);                 // scalar P1 space
  GridFunction temperature(vh);
  temperature = [](const Geometry::Point& p) { return p.x() + p.y(); };

  grid.add("temperature", temperature);
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-io-xdmf-fields-vector Vector fields

  Vector fields have multiple components per node. Create the finite element
  space with the number of components:

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 vh(mesh, 2);              // 2D vector P1 space
  GridFunction velocity(vh);
  velocity = [](const Geometry::Point& p)
  {
    return Math::Vector<Real>{{ -p.y(), p.x() }};
  };

  grid.add("velocity", velocity);
  @endcode

  @section guides-io-xdmf-patterns File patterns

  The XDMF writer uses customizable file naming patterns. The default
  patterns are:

  | File | Default Pattern | Example |
  |------|----------------|---------|
  | XDMF XML | `{stem}.xdmf` | `MyOutput.xdmf` |
  | Mesh HDF5 | `{stem}.{grid}.mesh.h5` | `MyOutput.mesh.h5` |
  | Field HDF5 | `{stem}.{grid}.{name}.{index}.h5` | `MyOutput.u.000000.h5` |

  Available placeholders:
  - `{stem}` — The base name passed to the XDMF constructor
  - `{grid}` — The name of the grid
  - `{name}` — The name of the field
  - `{index}` — The time step index
  - `{rank}` — The MPI rank (for distributed output)

  @section guides-io-xdmf-guide-paraview Opening files in ParaView

  To visualize XDMF output in ParaView:

  1. Open ParaView
  2. Use **File > Open** and select the `.xdmf` file
  3. Click **Apply** in the Properties panel
  4. Select a field from the dropdown menu to colorize the mesh
  5. For time-dependent data, use the time controls at the top

  @section guides-io-xdmf-example Complete example

  The @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson tutorial" derives the problem,
  solves it and exports the computed field. Its checked program is:

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-source

  @section guides-io-xdmf-see-also See also

  - @ref guides-io "I/O in Rodin overview"
  - @ref Rodin::IO::XDMF "XDMF class reference"
  - @ref examples-io-xdmf "XDMF example"
  - @ref examples-io-index "I/O examples"

  */
